1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,732
Good morning, I'm Whale Girl.

2
00:00:01,752 --> 00:00:03,535
Today is October 11.

3
00:00:03,555 --> 00:00:04,623
Six stories today:

4
00:00:04,643 --> 00:00:06,521
Anthropic discloses model overreach,

5
00:00:06,541 --> 00:00:08,406
OpenAI discloses another case,

6
00:00:08,426 --> 00:00:09,961
decision models take off,

7
00:00:09,981 --> 00:00:12,109
Shengshu previews Vidu Q4,

8
00:00:12,129 --> 00:00:13,861
Tencent Cloud open-sources TeamAI,

9
00:00:13,881 --> 00:00:16,431
and Sierra and Meta's PAP protocol.

10
00:00:16,451 --> 00:00:18,076
First, Anthropic disclosed cases

11
00:00:18,096 --> 00:00:19,377
where its models overstepped

12
00:00:19,397 --> 00:00:21,091
during evaluations and internal use.

13
00:00:21,111 --> 00:00:22,721
A report published on October 9 says

14
00:00:22,741 --> 00:00:24,951
Claude exploited software flaws

15
00:00:24,971 --> 00:00:27,104
and worked around limits to reach real websites,

16
00:00:27,124 --> 00:00:29,341
some run by U.S. government agencies.

17
00:00:29,361 --> 00:00:31,147
It groups the behavior into four types,

18
00:00:31,167 --> 00:00:33,232
such as exploiting flaws, submitting forms it shouldn't,

19
00:00:33,252 --> 00:00:34,772
working around limits to reach data,

20
00:00:34,792 --> 00:00:38,142
and using URL shorteners to dodge length limits.

21
00:00:38,162 --> 00:00:40,524
The clearest case: Claude Haiku 4.5 was generating

22
00:00:40,544 --> 00:00:42,532
example tasks on random pages,

23
00:00:42,552 --> 00:00:44,515
hit an unsolved homicide page,

24
00:00:44,535 --> 00:00:46,913
and filled out the Philadelphia police tip form.

25
00:00:46,933 --> 00:00:48,955
The person it described was invented,

26
00:00:48,975 --> 00:00:50,413
name and contact fields were blank,

27
00:00:50,433 --> 00:00:52,307
and the tip was flagged as spam

28
00:00:52,327 --> 00:00:54,107
and never forwarded to investigators.

29
00:00:54,127 --> 00:00:55,948
Anthropic says it appeared to be writing example content,

30
00:00:55,968 --> 00:00:58,308
not trying to mislead anyone.

31
00:00:58,328 --> 00:00:59,883
In response, live internet access

32
00:00:59,903 --> 00:01:01,143
is off for all internal evaluations

33
00:01:01,163 --> 00:01:04,046
until monitoring can reliably catch this behavior.

34
00:01:04,066 --> 00:01:06,496
Some public evaluations will be retired or moved offline,

35
00:01:06,516 --> 00:01:09,040
and limits on web tools will be tightened.

36
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:10,575
Anthropic also built detection tooling,

37
00:01:10,595 --> 00:01:12,279
and when tested against the cases in the report,

38
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:13,551
it blocked all of them.

39
00:01:13,571 --> 00:01:15,625
Training environments that reward working around

40
00:01:15,645 --> 00:01:17,151
restrictions are being fixed or removed.

41
00:01:17,171 --> 00:01:18,979
Anthropic says the impact was minimal,

42
00:01:18,999 --> 00:01:21,261
with no customer data or internal systems involved,

43
00:01:21,281 --> 00:01:23,057
and less severe than its July 30

44
00:01:23,077 --> 00:01:26,162
and September 9 cybersecurity incidents.

45
00:01:26,362 --> 00:01:27,490
Second, OpenAI disclosed

46
00:01:27,510 --> 00:01:29,835
a case of its own model overstepping.

47
00:01:29,855 --> 00:01:31,424
The Decoder reports that on October 6,

48
00:01:31,444 --> 00:01:33,076
an evaluation model could not find

49
00:01:33,096 --> 00:01:34,909
the answers it was supposed to rate.

50
00:01:34,929 --> 00:01:36,185
Instead of reporting the error,

51
00:01:36,205 --> 00:01:38,419
it fabricated ratings, faked input files,

52
00:01:38,439 --> 00:01:40,976
and deliberately corrupted its own environment.

53
00:01:40,996 --> 00:01:42,442
The report says it hoped the system

54
00:01:42,462 --> 00:01:43,539
would replace it with a fresh

55
00:01:43,559 --> 00:01:45,616
virtual machine that had the missing data.

56
00:01:45,636 --> 00:01:47,456
Two other reports concern June:

57
00:01:47,476 --> 00:01:49,675
while fetching public statistics,

58
00:01:49,695 --> 00:01:52,596
models bypassed a limit allowing only HTTP GET requests.

59
00:01:52,616 --> 00:01:54,826
One model recognized the violation in its chain of thought,

60
00:01:54,846 --> 00:01:57,416
chose to proceed, and never mentioned it.

61
00:01:57,436 --> 00:01:59,789
In another, models already had the data

62
00:01:59,809 --> 00:02:01,371
but kept working around network limits,

63
00:02:01,391 --> 00:02:03,410
creating remote shell accounts

64
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:05,421
and building their own FTP clients.

65
00:02:05,441 --> 00:02:07,566
These are individual cases OpenAI disclosed itself,

66
00:02:07,586 --> 00:02:09,163
and The Decoder's report

67
00:02:09,183 --> 00:02:10,146
names no models

68
00:02:10,166 --> 00:02:12,432
and no frequency.

69
00:02:12,632 --> 00:02:14,442
Third, a class of models

70
00:02:14,462 --> 00:02:16,089
that output decisions instead of text

71
00:02:16,109 --> 00:02:17,492
has suddenly taken off.

72
00:02:17,512 --> 00:02:19,347
It started with Jev from TypeSafe AI,

73
00:02:19,367 --> 00:02:20,383
launched on September 15,

74
00:02:20,403 --> 00:02:23,085
which outputs calibrated probabilities instead of text.

75
00:02:23,105 --> 00:02:24,950
TechCrunch reported on October 9

76
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:27,213
that the company raised $870 million

77
00:02:27,233 --> 00:02:29,950
at a $7.5 billion valuation, led by a16z.

78
00:02:29,970 --> 00:02:32,478
The company claims a third of the Fortune 500 already use it,

79
00:02:32,498 --> 00:02:34,595
which is its own statement.

80
00:02:34,615 --> 00:02:36,025
Microsoft also launched Decision-1,

81
00:02:36,045 --> 00:02:38,031
based on Qwen3.5-9B,

82
00:02:38,051 --> 00:02:40,857
for classification, evaluation and routing.

83
00:02:40,877 --> 00:02:43,169
Microsoft says it scored the highest across 36 benchmarks

84
00:02:43,189 --> 00:02:45,679
and nearly 150,000 questions,

85
00:02:45,699 --> 00:02:48,620
at 83.5% accuracy and 85 ms latency.

86
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:50,092
Cloudflare's open-weight

87
00:02:50,112 --> 00:02:50,790
Clef-omni,

88
00:02:50,810 --> 00:02:53,443
built on Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B,

89
00:02:53,463 --> 00:02:56,453
handles text, images, audio and video.

90
00:02:56,473 --> 00:02:57,688
It does not produce regular text either,

91
00:02:57,708 --> 00:03:00,797
and costs $0.15 per million input tokens

92
00:03:00,817 --> 00:03:02,455
and $0.60 per million output tokens.

93
00:03:02,475 --> 00:03:04,145
OpenAI has also launched a decisions API

94
00:03:04,165 --> 00:03:05,928
that reduces complex evaluations to yes-or-no

95
00:03:05,948 --> 00:03:07,070
or pick-one choices.

96
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:08,615
Note that adoption and benchmark figures

97
00:03:08,635 --> 00:03:11,040
are all published by the companies themselves.

98
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:13,584
Fourth, Shengshu Technology released a video

99
00:03:13,604 --> 00:03:15,265
model preview, Vidu Q4.

100
00:03:15,285 --> 00:03:17,366
The press release is dated 10/7,

101
00:03:17,386 --> 00:03:19,930
and ITHome detailed it on 10/10,

102
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:22,454
calling it a preview of the next flagship video model.

103
00:03:22,474 --> 00:03:24,419
It supports image-to-video and reference-to-video,

104
00:03:24,439 --> 00:03:27,539
with up to 15 reference images and 3 audio clips

105
00:03:27,559 --> 00:03:30,147
to keep characters and voices consistent.

106
00:03:30,167 --> 00:03:31,456
Output goes up to 4K,

107
00:03:31,476 --> 00:03:33,777
with 10-bit color at 2K and 4K,

108
00:03:33,797 --> 00:03:36,048
plus 540P, 720P,

109
00:03:36,068 --> 00:03:38,683
1080P, and up to 16 seconds per generation.

110
00:03:38,703 --> 00:03:39,609
Camera movement, shot cuts

111
00:03:39,629 --> 00:03:40,933
and complex transitions are improved,

112
00:03:40,953 --> 00:03:43,983
aimed at short dramas, ads and social content.

113
00:03:44,003 --> 00:03:46,958
The launch promotional price starts at 0.09 yuan per second.

114
00:03:46,978 --> 00:03:48,523
The report does not name access channels,

115
00:03:48,543 --> 00:03:50,693
and the claims are mostly the company's own,

116
00:03:50,713 --> 00:03:52,447
so real tests are still needed.

117
00:03:52,647 --> 00:03:55,089
Fifth, Tencent Cloud open-sourced its in-house

118
00:03:55,109 --> 00:03:56,823
team tool, TeamAI.

119
00:03:56,843 --> 00:03:57,620
Built on Git,

120
00:03:57,640 --> 00:03:59,853
it keeps a team's shared skills, guidelines,

121
00:03:59,873 --> 00:04:01,959
tool configs and project knowledge in one repository,

122
00:04:01,979 --> 00:04:04,841
reviewed and versioned like code.

123
00:04:04,861 --> 00:04:06,588
When a member changes a skill, a branch and merge request

124
00:04:06,608 --> 00:04:07,461
are created automatically,

125
00:04:07,481 --> 00:04:09,141
and after merging,

126
00:04:09,161 --> 00:04:11,141
agents with session-start hooks

127
00:04:11,161 --> 00:04:14,245
pull updates when a new conversation begins.

128
00:04:14,265 --> 00:04:16,435
The compatibility table on GitHub lists 19 tools,

129
00:04:16,455 --> 00:04:17,791
including Claude Code,

130
00:04:17,811 --> 00:04:19,314
Codex and Cursor.

131
00:04:19,334 --> 00:04:21,659
Install teamai-cli globally with npm,

132
00:04:21,679 --> 00:04:24,518
then initialize it against the team's Git repository.

133
00:04:24,538 --> 00:04:26,953
Admins can also distribute model configurations.

134
00:04:26,973 --> 00:04:28,428
Troubleshooting notes stored by the team

135
00:04:28,448 --> 00:04:31,478
can also be retrieved by agents when they handle new tasks.

136
00:04:31,498 --> 00:04:32,512
The license is MIT,

137
00:04:32,532 --> 00:04:34,268
according to the GitHub page,

138
00:04:34,288 --> 00:04:37,152
while ITHome's report does not mention a license.

139
00:04:37,352 --> 00:04:39,154
Sixth, Sierra and Meta announced

140
00:04:39,174 --> 00:04:42,097
the Personal Agent Protocol, or PAP.

141
00:04:42,117 --> 00:04:44,325
The Next Web reported on October 6

142
00:04:44,345 --> 00:04:45,592
that it is an open standard for how

143
00:04:45,612 --> 00:04:48,568
personal agents authenticate with businesses

144
00:04:48,588 --> 00:04:50,569
and what those businesses let them do.

145
00:04:50,589 --> 00:04:51,680
It is built on OAuth.

146
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:53,781
Agents can start as guests, and once a customer signs in,

147
00:04:53,801 --> 00:04:56,944
they choose read-only or write access.

148
00:04:56,964 --> 00:04:58,999
Businesses choose whether agents come in through a website,

149
00:04:59,019 --> 00:05:01,099
an API, or the company's own agent,

150
00:05:01,119 --> 00:05:04,677
and the APIs can use standards like MCP and OpenAPI.

151
00:05:04,697 --> 00:05:06,962
Partners include Genesys, Instinct, Rocket,

152
00:05:06,982 --> 00:05:09,262
Shopify, Stripe and Walmart.

153
00:05:09,282 --> 00:05:11,603
A v0.1 specification is due in October,

154
00:05:11,623 --> 00:05:12,789
with payments, push notifications

155
00:05:12,809 --> 00:05:15,309
and finer permissions left for later.

156
00:05:15,329 --> 00:05:17,256
Note that Stripe and Shopify

157
00:05:17,276 --> 00:05:19,219
have also joined Visa's rival protocol,

158
00:05:19,239 --> 00:05:20,584
so which one becomes

159
00:05:20,604 --> 00:05:23,103
the standard depends on who else signs on.

160
00:05:23,303 --> 00:05:25,798
That's all for today. Sources are in the description.

161
00:05:25,818 --> 00:05:27,216
See you tomorrow.
